<p>There is some interesting information that we can get by statically analyzing the source code. Here are them:</p>
<ol>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">$greet</code> variable is supposed to contain a name of a file; and we can control the value as this line of code shows:
    <div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre> if (isset($_GET['greet'])) $greet = $_GET['greet'];
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>If you get the reference, this challenge is based on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MpeaSNERwQA">this</a> video on Youtube by LiveOverflow. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">hash_hmac</code> function in that video is more or less the same with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">patched</code> function in this challenge, but supplying an array into the function would not work anymore because of this line of code:
    <div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
2
3
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre> if (is_string($string)) {
     ...
 }
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div>    </div>
    <p>Anyway, if you don’t understand what I’m talking about, please watch the video, it’s all there :).</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">patched</code> function validates our input and returns a message-digested hash value from the input. The difference between this and the function in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MpeaSNERwQA">that</a> video is: with the intention of patching (or should I say securing) the application, this challenge uses <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">hash</code> function instead of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">hash_hmac</code> function to generate the hash value. By doing that, the application is potentially vulnerable to <em>Hash Length Extension Attack</em>. We’ll come back to this very soon.</p>
  </li>
  <li>This line of code:
    <div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
</pre></td><td class="rouge-code"><pre> $greet = filter_var($greet, FILTER_UNSAFE_RAW, FILTER_FLAG_STRIP_LOW|FILTER_FLAG_STRIP_HIGH);
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div>    </div>
    <p>basically strips out every special character from the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">$greet</code> variable.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>If the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mac</code> we provide (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">$_GET['mac'</code>) is not equals to the generated <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mac</code> value, the application is terminated. So we need to make sure that both are the same.</p>
  </li>
